r\nDr Martin Zarka\nAssociate Professor Nazif Alic\nEngage with us\nImpact\nPaper of the Month\nIndustry engagement\nWork with us\nNews & events\nNews\nEvents\nHome\nAbout us\nDirectory\nContact us\nProfessional and technical staff\nSchool executive team\nAcademic and research leaders\nAcademic affiliates\nEducation focused academics\nEquity, Diversity & Inclusion\nStudy areas\nBioinformatics\nBiotechnology\nGenetics\nMicrobiology\nStudent life & resources\nStudent resources\nCourses\nHonours\nIndustry experience\nStudent testimonials\nLiam Cheney: internships and opportunities\nJayne Liu: BABS international student to intern to job\nAshton Curry-Hyde: Postgraduate Research Intern Program (APR.Intern)\nClubs & societies\nPostgraduate\nDoctor of Philosophy\nMasters by Research\nMaster of Philosophy\nBABS HDR Information Booklet\nScholarships\nUndergraduate scholarships\nHonours Indigenous Scholarship\nPostgraduate scholarships\nGraduate stories\nLife in the extremes\nMicrobes on Mars\nSharing breakthroughs\nEntrepreneurship for good\nResearch and innovation\nDemocratising science\nFrom the microscope to the ministry\nStart-up success\nOur research\nFacilities & services\nVafaee Lab\nGenomics and bioinformatics\nGene regulation\nSystems biology\nNeurogenomics\nMicrobiology and microbiomes\nMicrobes in health & disease\nMicrobes in the environment\nMolecular and cell biology\nMetabolism & metabolic disorders\nSynthetic biology\nStructural Biology\nPhD stories\nDr Cristy Gelling\nProfessor David Sinclair\nDr Jacinta Flattery-O\u2019Brien\nDr Mukan Ji\nDr Matt Piper\nDr Martin Zarka\nAssociate Professor Nazif Alic\nEngage with us\nImpact\nPaper of the Month\nIndustry engagement\nWork with us\nNews & events\nNews\nEvents\nCarve your path in the modern life sciences\nWhen you study with us, you'll study with one of Australia\u2019s most innovative research hubs. Discover your future at the forefront of biotech and biomolecular science.\nLearn more\nPersonalise\nShow filters\nHide filters\nMost employable students\n2020\u20132026 AFR Top100 Future Leaders & Graduate Employers Awards.\n19th in the world\n2024\u20132027 QS World University Rankings.\nA research powerhouse\n101st globally, 47th in the Asia Pacific Region, and 2nd in Australia in the 2023 Nature Index Tables.\nEnquire now\nApply now\nAbout us\nStudy areas\nBioinformatics\nBioinformatics develops technologies for analysing and interpreting genetic information. It merges computer science and statistics, applying them to the goals of molecular biology.\nopens in a new window\nBiotechnology\nBiotechnology offers an exciting future with an impact on wide ranging industries. It\u2019s used in the production of food, pharmaceuticals, industrial chemicals, crop improvement and more.\nopens in a new window\nGenetics\nThe building blocks of life, genetics is the study of gene behaviour and heredity that is fundamental to all living organisms. Discover where a degree specialising in genetics can take you.\nopens in a new window\nMicrobiology\nMicrobiology is the scientific study of the smallest forms of life - bacteria, viruses, archaea, fungi and protozoa. These fascinating organisms impact on our lives in many ways.\nopens in a new window\nMolecular and cell biology\nSet to have an increasingly critical role in modern medicine, genetics and evolutionary biology, choose a future-proofed degree with this blend of biochemistry, microbiology and cell biology.\nopens in a new window\nStudy with us\nUndergraduate\nChoose your pathway within BABS with our multitude of degree programs and specialisations. Your learning will be heightened by our exceptional academics, facilities and industry links.\nPostgraduate\nEnhance your education or push new frontiers in science with our postgraduate and postgraduate research degrees, from Graduate Certificates to PhDs.\nResearch & impact\nOur research is divided into three clusters. Each cluster has different strengths.\nGenomics and Bioinformatics\nWe support forward-thinking research by focusing on the key areas of gene regulation, systems biology and neurogenomics; specifically, the study of human disease, gene expression, proteomics, biological systems and their functions, and neurobiology from a genomic perspective.\nMicrobiology and Microbiomes\nWith a passion for discovery, our research centres on synthetic biology and metabolism and molecular cell biology. We incorporate molecular genetics, stem cell biology, microscopy and more to answer unsolved biological questions and train the next generation of life scientists.\nMolecular and Cell Biology\nWe aim to unravel the mechanisms behind ubiquitous microbes and their vital function in every life process. Our research in microbiology and microbiomes explores the importance of microbes in the environment and microbial contributions to health and disease.\nLearn more\nMeet our people\nAcademic and research leaders\nSchool executive team\nEducation focused academics\nOur people\nWays to connect\nEnquire now\nTop\nunsw.edu.au\nEngage with us\nContact us\nFind an expert\nCareers at UNSW\nEducation at UNSW\nAlumni\nStudy\nUNSW International\nDegree Finder\nStudent Portal\nAcademic Calendar\nNews, Media & Events\nNewsroom\nUNSW events\nStudent news\nAbout us\nOur rankings & reputation\nFaculties and schools\nCampus locations\nCentres and institutes\nUNSW Library\nUNSW\nSydney NSW 2033 Australia\nTelephone: +61 2 93851000\nUNSW CRICOS Provider Code: 00098G\nTEQSA Provider ID: PRV12055\nABN: 57 195 873 179\nAcknowledgement of Country\nUNSW respectfully acknowledges the Bidjigal, Biripai, Dharug, Gadigal, Gumbaynggirr, Ngunnawal and Wiradjuri peoples, on whose unceded lands we are privileged to learn, teach and work. We honour the Elders of these Nations, past and present, and recognise the broader Nations with whom we walk together. UNSW acknowledges the enduring connection of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples to culture, community and Country.\nThe Uluru Statement\nFollow us\nFollow us\nFollow UNSW on LinkedIn\nFollow UNSW on Facebook\nFollow UNSW on Instagram\nFollow UNSW on TikTok\nFollow UNSW on YouTube\nFollow UNSW on WeChat\nUNSW Little Red Book\nPrivacy statement\nCopyright & Disclaimer\nAccessibility\nSite feedback\nComplaints", "links": [["Skip to Main Content", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs#main-content"], ["", "https://www.unsw.edu.au"], ["Study", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/study"], ["Research", "https://research.unsw.edu.au/"], ["Faculties", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/about-us/excellence/faculties-schools"], ["Apply", "https://www.unsw.edu.au"], ["About", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/about-us"], ["News", "https://newsroom.unsw.edu.au"], ["Events", "https://www.events.unsw.edu.au/"], ["myUNSW", "https://my.unsw.edu.au"], ["Staff intranet", "https://unsw.sharepoint.com/sites/Science-staff-intranet/"], ["Alumni & Giving", "https://www.alumni.giving.unsw.edu.au/alumni"], ["", "https://www.unsw.edu.au"], ["Science", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science"], ["Our schools", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools"], ["Follow", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/socialmedia"], ["LinkedIn", "https://www.linkedin.com/school/unsw/"], ["Facebook", "https://www.facebook.com/unsw"], ["Instagram", "https://www.instagram.com/unsw/"], ["TikTok", "https://www.tiktok.com/@unsw"], ["YouTube", "https://www.youtube.com/unsw"], ["Science", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science"], ["Biotechnology & Biomolecular Sciences", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs"], ["Home", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs/home"], ["About us", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs/about-us"], ["Directory", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs/about-us/directory"], ["Contact us", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs/about-us/contact-us"], ["Professional and technical staff", "https://www.unsw.edu.au/science/our-schools/babs/about-us/professional-and-technical-staff"], ["School execut